<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Arakan (Lemro period)</h1><p class="st-meta">818 CE – 1406 CE (approx.) · capital Launggyet</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Six centuries of Arakanese kingdoms in the Lemro river valley, between the fall of Waithali and the founding of Mrauk U.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Rakhine chronicles date the start of the Lemro period to 818, when King Khittathin founded Pyinsa on the Lemro river after Waithali's decline. Over the following centuries the capital shifted between several towns in the valley, including Parein, Hkrit, Pyinsa again and finally Launggyet, founded in 1251.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Lemro kingdoms ruled the Rakhine coast through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> trade, with strong links to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom</a>, which at times claimed overlordship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> became dominant, and kings built pagodas and patronised monks. Political history was turbulent, with frequent succession disputes and invasions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1406 forces of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ava-kingdom">Ava Kingdom</a> drove King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/min-saw-mon">Min Saw Mon</a> from Launggyet into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. With help from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate</a> he regained the throne in 1429 and the next year founded a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mrauk-u">Mrauk U</a>, opening the period of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mrauk-u-kingdom">Mrauk U Kingdom</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-arakan-lemro-period.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Arakan (Lemro period)" width="264" height="583"><figcaption>Image: Hybernator · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burma_c._1310.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>818 CE – 1406 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Launggyet</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khittathin, Min Saw Mon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arakanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lemro kingdoms, Pyinsa, Launggyet Kingdom, Launggyet</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1112&amp;focus=kingdom-of-arakan-lemro-period" aria-label="Kingdom of Arakan (Lemro period) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7191 1417 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Arakan (Lemro period) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7511 1673L7509 1672M7511 1673L7550 1651M7511 1673L7574 1626M7511 1673L7509 1672"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7509 1672h0M7550 1651h0M7574 1626h0M7509 1672h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7511 1673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7511 1673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1112&amp;focus=kingdom-of-arakan-lemro-period">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
